  "interpretation": "Removing near-duplicates moved intent macro-F1 by -0.0006 and behaviour by -0.0057 over 622 records. On dependence, the two heads' fusion inputs share a normalised mutual information of 0.3287, and the behaviour head fires on 1.00% of benign records. Both bear directly on the noisy-OR independence assumption in fusion.py.",
  "limitations": [
    "This is SMS text, not call transcripts. Both heads are evaluated on the register they were trained on; conversational speech transcribed by ASR is a different distribution and is not measured here.",
    "Records are scored from ground-truth text, not from ASR output. In the live pipeline these heads read a transcript, so real end-to-end accuracy is bounded by ASR accuracy as well (docs/BLOCKERS.md P4).",
    "Intent labels remain 100% collinear with the corpus scam flag (O10). A high intent macro-F1 partly measures the easier scam/not-scam boundary and must not be presented as intent-discrimination accuracy.",
    "No Tamil records exist, so neither head is evaluated in Tamil at all. Tamil remains UNSUPPORTED_LANGUAGE (O11).",
    "The de-leaked split removes records whose digits-masked key matches a training record. That over-removes slightly, so the de-leaked figure is a conservative lower bound rather than an exact correction."
  ],
  "conclusion": "Quote the de-leaked figures, with support counts, and never quote OTP_REQUEST. The dependence measurement determines whether noisy-OR is double-counting one signal; it feeds the fusion ablation rather than being acted on here.",
  "configuration": {
    "min_support_floor": 30,
    "behavior_threshold": 0.5,
    "max_length": 96
  },
  "runtime_sec": 1037.3
